  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Pontic Greek music tradition of the Pontic Greek ethnic community originating in the Black Sea coast of Anatolia (Trabzon, Sinop, Samsun regions, now Turkey), displaced post-1923 Greek-Turkish population exchange to mainland Greece (especially northern Greece, Athens, and Thessaloniki). Performance configuration centers on the Pontic kemenche (three-string upright-fiddle, distinct from Cretan lyra in tuning and playing technique), often accompanied by daouli large frame-drum and angeion bagpipe. Repertoire features fast asymmetrical-meter dance-music (omal, tik, dipat, tromakton) and slow narrative-and-lament forms (Pontic dialect of Greek with characteristic Anatolian-Greek phonological features). Distinguished from broader Greek demotic music by Pontic-Black-Sea regional identity, kemenche-instrument signature, and post-1923 diaspora-cultural-memory orientation.",
